{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfhpp8HMd8dcrMH5q1yF1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `bayesian_chance_vs_repeating_motif` and it passes the admission self-check (`OK`). Its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO is -1002.4, against the incumbent's -1021.6. That figure comes from a 100-draw, 1-chain test, so take it as a hint, not a result.\n\n**What I changed in `bayesian_overalternating_chance_model`:** I added one more \"regular\" generator. The model already compares a fair coin (one people believe switches too often) against two regular explanations: a coin that tends to switch or repeat, and a coin biased towards heads or tails. The new third explanation is a repeating-motif process. It picks a short pattern of 1–4 flips (e.g. H, HT, HHT, HHTT) and keeps copying it, with an occasional slip at a fitted rate. Sequences that nearly repeat a short pattern, such as perfect alternation and period-3/4 patterns, now count as regular and look less random. This targets two of the critique's findings that survive the false-discovery correction: the incumbent under-penalises perfect alternation (people chose it 0.768 of the time, the model predicted 0.857) and under-penalises periodic motifs (0.46 against 0.509).\n\n**Speed:** my first version computed the regular-generator likelihood for every trial. It would have needed about 19 min per chain at production settings, too close to the 30-min admission limit. I rewrote it to compute that likelihood once per distinct sequence and look it up for each trial, which brings it to about 9 min per chain.\n\n**Risks for full admission:**\n- **Unreliable ELPD:** the smoke fit flags 11.9% of trials as having unreliable PSIS-LOO estimates (Pareto k > 0.5), well over the 1% tolerance. That is common with only 100 draws, but if it persists in the full fit, the model's ELPD would be marked untrustworthy.\n- **Convergence untested:** the check can't measure R-hat from a single chain. In a 600-iteration test the person-to-person spread in sensitivity came out at about 1.13, much wider than the 0.5-scale prior the incumbent uses. That suggests people differ a lot in how decisively they choose.\n- **Missing columns:** if a row has no `participant_id` or `chose_left`, the code fills in 0 instead of raising an error. I did this so the model still runs on stimulus pools without those columns, but it is a silent default.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_4/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":738,"cache_read_input_tokens":67839,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":738,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a77e3312-5f3e-4143-8073-8714b30978fa","uuid":"d3651a51-66c0-416a-8876-0214309a691d","timestamp":"2026-10-04T20:26:22.529Z","request_id":"req_011Cfhpp6CqychzFQMua1CZn"}
